Usage
=====

This plugin applies a new skin to the deployed site of the latest release of a project.

Before you begin, make sure the current project already uses the new skin. It must be a skin that Doxia 2 renders,
such as fluido 2.x.

When you run `siteskinner:skin`, the plugin:

* looks for the most recent released version of the project and reads the SCM connection from its POM;
* checks out the sources of that version under `target/siteskinner`;
* merges the skin, the layout settings and the `custom` section of the current project's `site.xml` into the checked
  out `site.xml`, written in the Doxia 2 site model;
* runs `mvn site` on the checked out project. *Et voilà*, we have the old content with a new skin.

Check the site under `target/siteskinner/target/site`, then deploy it with `mvn site:deploy` from
`target/siteskinner`.

The released project is rendered with its own maven-site-plugin, which must be based on Doxia 2: 3.20.0 or later.
Releases built with an older site plugin cannot be re-skinned, and the plugin stops before the checkout is rendered.

This plugin supports locales and multi-module projects (the checkout will only be done on the root of the project).

Command line options
====================

The skinner plugin has only a few options:

* `siteDeploy` (default: **false**): If this value is set to true the re-skinned site will immediately be deployed.
* `forceCheckout` (default: **false**): By setting this value to `true` a clean checkout is done instead of an update.
* `mergeBody` (default: **true**): By setting this value to `false` none of the body elements will be merged, so the original layout is preserved.
* `arguments` : additional arguments for `mvn site`; only `-D`, `-P` and `-X` are supported.
* `mavenHome` : the Maven installation that renders the released site; defaults to the Maven running the plugin.

```sh
 mvn siteskinner:skin -DmavenHome=/path/to/maven-version
```

Publish date
============

The re-rendered site shows the original publish date: the `publishDate` parameter when set, otherwise the date the
released artifact was built or deployed. Doxia 2 skins such as fluido 2.x show the date that maven-site-plugin takes
from `project.build.outputTimestamp`, so the plugin passes that property to the site build. A release whose own POM sets
`project.build.outputTimestamp`, not only its parent, keeps that value unless `publishDate` is set. The date is also written into the
`custom/publishDate` element of the released `site.xml`, for skins that read it.
